This study examines a one-dimensional transport equation characterized by nonlocal flux and periodic conditions, with no dissipative effects, on the domain $(-1,1)$. The equation is defined as \begin{align*}
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} u(x,t) + \int_{-1}^{1} p(x-y)u(y,t)dy = 0, \quad x \in -1,1), \quad t > 0,
\end{align*}with periodic boundary conditions and the specific flux kernel $p$ satisfying \begin{align*}
\int_{-1}^{1} |p(x)|dx < \infty, \quad \int_{-1}^{1} p(x)dx = 0.
\end{align*}The problem is considered for initial conditions $u_0 \in H^s_{odd}(-1,1)$ with $s \geq 0$, and the problem seeks to analyze the long-time behavior of solutions. Our main contributions include establishing local and global well-posedness results, and demonstrating that solutions with smooth, odd initial conditions eventually become zero in a global sense. Additionally, we demonstrate that this equilibrium state is stable. Notably, we identify a frequency decomposition and a long-wave approximation, and provide an accurate description of the long-time dynamics through an accurate closure model. This model highlights an exponential decay rate in a particular variable, and demonstrates that this decay rate can be controlled through adjusting the dispersion part of the kernel. This study is motivated by an analysis of a related equation appearing in an earlier study. The method used here may also be applicable to other similar equations. In particular, it is worthwhile to investigate the case where the flux kernel has real roots, as indicated by the provided analysis. Furthermore, this study attempts to improve the limiting approach described in the referenced study, and offers an alternative perspective on the observed decay rate. Additionally, it is interesting to consider the case where the flux kernel has a non-symmetric form, as indicated by the provided analysis. The method used here may also be applicable to other similar equations. In particular, it is worthwhile to investigate the case where the flux kernel has real roots, as indicated by the provided analysis. The method used here may also be applicable to other similar equations. In particular, it is worthwhile to investigate the case where the flux kernel has real roots, as indicated by the provided analysis. The method used here may also be applicable to other similar equations
